{"version":"6.9","hitCount":2444,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQSpPiyg1NTEyNjA0MA==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=((\"conversational AI\" OR chatbot OR \"AI agent\" OR \"conversational agent\") AND (\"large language model\" OR LLM OR \"generative AI\") AND (governance OR oversight OR accountability)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]&cursorMark=AoIIQSpPiyg1NTEyNjA0MA==&resultType=core&pageSize=100&format=json","request":{"queryString":"((\"conversational AI\" OR chatbot OR \"AI agent\" OR \"conversational agent\") AND (\"large language model\" OR LLM OR \"generative AI\") AND (governance OR oversight OR accountability)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":100,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42293851","source":"MED","pmid":"42293851","pmcid":"PMC13263806","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13263806"]},"doi":"10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097","title":"Large language models for post-discharge follow-up in erectile dysfunction care: a narrative review.","authorString":"Zhang Y, Lv B, Xu R, Shi C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yiman","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lv B","firstName":"Bodong","lastName":"Lv","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu R","firstName":"Runnan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi C","firstName":"Chenghao","lastName":"Shi","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4217778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Translational andrology and urology","medlineAbbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","isoabbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","nlmid":"101581119","essn":"2223-4691","issn":"2223-4691"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"185","abstractText":"<h4>Background and objective</h4>Erectile dysfunction (ED) is common and closely linked to cardiometabolic disease, psychological distress, and relationship problems. Although effective treatments are available, outcomes depend on sustained post-discharge follow-up to assess response, adherence, adverse effects, and psychosocial needs. In practice, ED follow-up is often hindered by stigma, embarrassment, limited access, and fragmented communication. This narrative review examines the potential role, risks, and implementation requirements of large language model (LLM)-assisted follow-up in ED care.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a targeted literature search in PubMed and Embase up to 15 January 2026, supplemented by Google Scholar for citation tracking. We selected clinically relevant evidence and conceptual work on ED follow-up, conversational artificial intelligence (AI), and safety governance, and synthesized findings thematically.<h4>Key content and findings</h4>Among 280 included sources, only 10 were ED-specific, while 270 provided transferable evidence from chronic disease, oncology, and mental health follow-up settings. LLMs may reduce communication barriers, enable low-threshold symptom check-ins, reinforce discharge education, and collect patient-reported outcomes more frequently than traditional follow-up. Potential clinical value is greatest for structured, low-risk tasks such as education reinforcement, adherence support, and routine symptom monitoring. However, ED follow-up includes medication safety, nuanced counseling, mental health and relationship contexts, and sensitive data governance. Key risks include inaccurate or unsafe recommendations, privacy breaches, inappropriate handling of mental health red flags, and unclear accountability. A risk-stratified, human-in-the-loop implementation model can balance feasibility and safety by reserving autonomous LLM interactions for low-risk content while triggering clinician review and escalation for predefined high-risk scenarios.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-assisted follow-up could become a useful adjunct in ED care if implemented with clear boundaries, auditability, privacy protection, and robust human oversight. A nurse-supervised, trigger-based governance model, which aligns with current follow-up workflows, may enhance continuity and responsiveness without compromising clinical accountability. Prospective evaluations should prioritize patient acceptability, safety outcomes, workload impact, and implementation feasibility.","affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Erectile dysfunction (ED)","Human-in-the-loop","Post-discharge Follow-up","Large Language Model (Llm)","Conversational Artificial Intelligence (Conversational Ai)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-26"},{"id":"PMC13296990","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC13296990","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13296990"]},"title":"Beyond accuracy: how generative artificial intelligence reshapes the foundations of public health discourse","journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"36","journalIssueId":4214586,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"European journal of public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Eur J Public Health","nlmid":"9204966","essn":"1464-360X","issn":"1101-1262"}},"pubYear":"2026","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comment","discussion"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC13296990/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13296990?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42170120","source":"MED","pmid":"42170120","pmcid":"PMC13189089","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13189089"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107391","title":"Conversational Artificial Intelligence in Medical Education: A Scoping Review.","authorString":"Aktar T, Farquhar RJ, Jacobs C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Aktar T","firstName":"Tasniya","lastName":"Aktar","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9838-3967"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Farquhar RJ","firstName":"Robert J","lastName":"Farquhar","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7028-112X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Jacobs C","firstName":"Chris","lastName":"Jacobs","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, University of Bath, Bath, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Postgraduate Medical Education, Great Western Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, Swindon, GBR."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9838-3967"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7028-112X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107391","abstractText":"Conversational artificial intelligence (AI), encompassing chatbots and large language models (LLMs), is rapidly emerging in the sphere of medical education as a dynamic tool for interactive learning. By generating realistic dialogue, simulating patient encounters, and providing adaptive feedback, these systems create new possibilities for learners to practise communication, clinical reasoning, and decision-making in a flexible and accessible way. Yet, despite increasing enthusiasm, evidence regarding their educational value remains fragmented. This scoping review examines the existing literature on conversational AI in undergraduate medical education, focusing on three key domains: educational utility, technology usability, and fidelity. A comprehensive search was conducted across PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science in August 2025. After deduplication, 496 unique studies were screened, and 20 met the inclusion criteria. These studies employed diverse methodologies and evaluation approaches. Methodological rigour was assessed using a validated framework designed for medical education research.  Across the literature, conversational AI demonstrates considerable potential to enhance engagement, support self-directed learning, and expand access to experiential practice. Learners generally view these systems as intuitive and motivating, and many studies suggest benefits for clinical reasoning and communication training. However, limitations in reliability, realism, and technical accuracy persist, and outcome measures remain inconsistent. Few studies assess the impact of response latency or the long-term transfer of skills to clinical settings, and methodological rigour is often modest. Overall, conversational AI appears to be a promising adjunct to traditional medical teaching rather than a replacement. Its value lies in scalability, interactivity, and adaptability, but effective integration requires thoughtful design, validated evaluation frameworks, and ongoing human oversight. As technology advances, further research should focus on standardising assessment methods, exploring learning outcomes beyond user satisfaction, and addressing fidelity and responsiveness to ensure meaningful, safe, and sustainable implementation within modern medical curricula.","affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence In Medicine","Medical Education Technology","Chatbots","Medical Education Curriculum","Conversational Artificial Intelligence"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107391"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189089"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189089?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-20"},{"id":"42194487","source":"MED","pmid":"42194487","pmcid":"PMC13205439","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13205439"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14101395","title":"Safety Mechanisms and Risk Mitigation in Generative AI Mental Health Chatbots: A Systematic Scoping Review.","authorString":"Olisaeloka L, Richardson CG, Wang AY, Munthali RJ, Vigo DV.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Olisaeloka L","firstName":"Lotenna","lastName":"Olisaeloka","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Richardson CG","firstName":"Chris G","lastName":"Richardson","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang AY","firstName":"Angel Y","lastName":"Wang","initials":"AY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Munthali RJ","firstName":"Richard J","lastName":"Munthali","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Vigo DV","firstName":"Daniel V","lastName":"Vigo","initials":"DV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4201779,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1395","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative AI (GenAI) mental health chatbots are increasingly being developed to help address persistent barriers to mental healthcare. Unlike earlier rule-based and retrieval-based systems, GenAI chatbots generate open-ended outputs that can be inaccurate and unsafe. Documented harms from general-purpose GenAI chatbots have highlighted the need for purpose-built interventions with dedicated safeguards, yet how safety is implemented in such interventions remains poorly understood.<h4>Methods</h4>This scoping review followed the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology and PRISMA-ScR guidelines, with a prospectively registered and peer-reviewed protocol. A systematic search of seven academic databases and search engines including MEDLINE, Scopus, PsycINFO, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Google Scholar and Consensus was conducted in July 2025. Two reviewers independently screened records and extracted data. Safety mechanisms and risk mitigation strategies were narratively synthesised across three pre-specified domains: technical safeguards, pre-deployment safety considerations, and delivery-phase risk mitigation strategies.<h4>Results</h4>Twenty-one studies across 11 countries were included. Most interventions incorporated at least one technical safety mechanism, most commonly fine-tuning and prompt engineering. A smaller subset implemented layered safety architectures combining retrieval systems, content filters or risk classifiers, and rule-based algorithms. Pre-deployment safeguards included clinical expert and user co-design approaches, research ethics procedures, and data privacy measures. During intervention delivery, detailed onboarding with role clarification was common, but human oversight was limited. Crisis referral protocols varied in rigour but were mostly underdeveloped, and systematic adverse event monitoring was sparse. Documented safety failures included missed suicidal ideation and provision of inaccurate clinical information.<h4>Conclusions</h4>GenAI chatbot interventions require a robust sociotechnical approach that integrates technical safeguards with user co-design, procedural controls, and human oversight. Future research is needed to evaluate efficacy, improve safeguards and standardise safety outcome measurement. Regulatory oversight proportional to the risks these systems carry is required to enable integration into stepped or blended mental healthcare.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Mental Health Chatbots","Ai Safety","Generative Ai","Chatgpt","Ai Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14101395"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1174289","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42212181","source":"MED","pmid":"42212181","pmcid":"PMC13212166","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212166"]},"doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s581251","title":"Cybersecurity and Privacy Risks of Generative AI Mental-Health Chatbots: A Systematic Review and Regulatory Framework.","authorString":"Sawesi S, Sabbineni H, Shagamreddy RR, Rashrash B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sawesi S","firstName":"Suhila","lastName":"Sawesi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sabbineni H","firstName":"Hemalatha","lastName":"Sabbineni","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shagamreddy RR","firstName":"Rajashekhar Reddy","lastName":"Shagamreddy","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rashrash B","firstName":"Budur","lastName":"Rashrash","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, West Virginia School of Osteopathic Medicine, Lewisburg, WV, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691","essn":"1178-2390","issn":"1178-2390"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"581251","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Large language models (LLMs) and other generative artificial intelligence systems are increasingly used in mental health care for psychoeducation, emotional support, screening, and crisis-related interactions. To our knowledge, this is the first structured synthesis explicitly mapping LLM-specific cybersecurity and privacy risks to Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) regulatory frameworks. We aimed to characterize deployment patterns, identify multi-layered risks, and evaluate alignment of reported safeguards with established healthcare governance standards.<h4>Methods</h4>A PRISMA-guided systematic review was conducted using PubMed, APA PsycNet, and Google Scholar. After screening eligible records against predefined inclusion criteria, 33 studies were included. Two reviewers independently extracted data on application domains, deployment settings, risk categories, attack surfaces, data sensitivity, and reported or recommended controls.<h4>Results</h4>Generative AI chatbots were most frequently used for therapy or emotional support (13/33, 39.4%), followed by safety evaluation or benchmarking (9/33, 27.3%) and psychoeducation or advice (6/33, 18.2%). Suicide prevention or crisis detection was the most common domain (10/33, 30.3%). Most systems relied on general-purpose LLMs (21/33, 63.6%) and were deployed via consumer-facing platforms (16/33, 48.5%). Key risks included harmful or unsafe outputs, failures in crisis response, exposure of sensitive personal information, and limited transparency. Critically, 78.8% of studies (26/33) were rated high risk for cybersecurity evaluation rigor, indicating that formal adversarial testing and structured threat modeling remain rare.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Current governance frameworks have not fully adapted to generative conversational AI in mental health contexts. Because the therapeutic interface functions as a primary attack surface, single-layer security evaluation (assessing only software validation or content safety in isolation) is inadequate. More comprehensive approaches are needed, including stronger cybersecurity controls, privacy-preserving data practices, and explicit alignment with FDA SaMD guidance, HIPAA, ISO 14971, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.","affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk management","Clinical Safety","Data Protection","Regulatory Frameworks","Digital Therapeutics","Conversational Systems"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S581251"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"42211192","source":"MED","pmid":"42211192","pmcid":"PMC13212461","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212461"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1847854","title":"Conversational AI for perinatal mental health: promise, limits, and a human-AI stepped-care framework.","authorString":"Yang H, Lin DM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang H","firstName":"Haiyan","lastName":"Yang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reproductive Medicine Center, The First Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University, Wenzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin DM","firstName":"Dong-Mei","lastName":"Lin","initials":"DM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Third Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University (Ruian People's Hospital), Wenzhou, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112086,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in psychiatry","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Psychiatry","isoabbreviation":"Front Psychiatry","nlmid":"101545006","essn":"1664-0640","issn":"1664-0640"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1847854","abstractText":"Perinatal mental health conditions are common yet persistently underdetected and undertreated. Recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (AI) and multimodal interaction offer new opportunities for scalable, continuous support, making the concept of the \"digital doula\" particularly compelling. Because perinatal care relies heavily on emotional support, repeated contact, and timely escalation, conversational AI is attractive as a potential adjunct to existing care. We argue that digital doulas should not be framed as autonomous substitutes for clinicians or human doulas, but rather conceptualized as AI-enabled relational interfaces embedded within a stepped-care model. We propose four core functions for digital doulas: companionship, symptom interpretation, navigation, and sentinel monitoring. These roles can reduce disclosure barriers, extend support between clinical visits, and strengthen linkage to appropriate care. However, conversational AI also introduces significant risks, including the illusion of empathy, crisis recognition failures, algorithmic bias against vulnerable populations, data privacy vulnerabilities, and unsafe deployment. To address these risks, we outline a safety-by-design agenda emphasizing human oversight, clear escalation protocols with medico-legal accountability, workflow integration, and equity-centered evaluation. Ultimately, the clinical value of digital doulas depends on safely strengthening the perinatal mental health care cascade without replacing its core human relationships.","affiliation":"Reproductive Medicine Center, The First Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University, Wenzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Stepped Care","Postpartum Depression","Perinatal Mental Health","Conversational Ai","Generative Ai","Digital Doula"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1847854"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212461"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212461?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-13"},{"id":"41756591","source":"MED","pmid":"41756591","pmcid":"PMC12933587","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12933587"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.jpra.2026.01.048","title":"Correspondence on \"Artificial intelligence as a surgical advisor before a DIEP breast reconstruction. A blinded comparative study of three large language models\".","authorString":"Mathieu O, Goutard M, Honart JF, Leymarie N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mathieu O","firstName":"Olivier","lastName":"Mathieu","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-9599-1685"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Gustave Roussy Cancer Campus, 114 Rue Edouard Vaillant, 94800, Villejuif, France."},{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Hôpital Saint-Louis, Assistance Publique - Hôpitaux de Paris, 1 Avenue Claude Vellefaux, 75010, Paris, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Goutard M","firstName":"Marion","lastName":"Goutard","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Gustave Roussy Cancer Campus, 114 Rue Edouard Vaillant, 94800, Villejuif, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Honart JF","firstName":"Jean-François","lastName":"Honart","initials":"JF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Gustave Roussy Cancer Campus, 114 Rue Edouard Vaillant, 94800, Villejuif, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Leymarie N","firstName":"Nicolas","lastName":"Leymarie","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Gustave Roussy Cancer Campus, 114 Rue Edouard Vaillant, 94800, Villejuif, France."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-9599-1685"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"48","journalIssueId":4068413,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JPRAS open","medlineAbbreviation":"JPRAS Open","isoabbreviation":"JPRAS Open","nlmid":"101680420","essn":"2352-5878","issn":"2352-5878"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"988-990","affiliation":"Department of Plastic Surgery, Gustave Roussy Cancer Campus, 114 Rue Edouard Vaillant, 94800, Villejuif, France.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpra.2026.01.048"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12933587"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12933587?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-04"},{"id":"42359442","source":"MED","pmid":"42359442","pmcid":"PMC13290728","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13290728"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1830254","title":"Artificial intelligence in undergraduate medical education clinical skills curricula: a scoping review of implementations since 2022.","authorString":"Thind BS, Javidi D, Schwartz LM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thind BS","firstName":"Birpartap S","lastName":"Thind","initials":"BS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Javidi D","firstName":"Daryoush","lastName":"Javidi","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Education, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Schwartz LM","firstName":"Lisa M","lastName":"Schwartz","initials":"LM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Education, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1830254","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>To systematically identify and synthesize peer-reviewed literature describing implemented AI innovations within undergraduate medical education clinical skills curricula from January 2022 through January 2026.<h4>Method</h4>The authors conducted a scoping review querying PubMed and Scopus, supplemented by SciSpace as an AI-assisted citation discovery tool. Eligible studies described utilizing AI to deliver the clinical skills curriculum in innovative ways (e.g., instruction in history-taking, communication, clinical reasoning, clinical documentation, OSCE/simulation assessment). We extracted data into standardized templates and thematically sorted to characterize how AI-assisted instruction was being implemented across educational objectives.<h4>Results</h4>From 1,130 initial records, 39 studies met inclusion criteria. AI-assisted instruction clustered into eight thematic categories: LLM-Based Virtual Patient and Clinical Simulation Systems (<i>n</i> = 19), AI-Augmented OSCE and Simulation Assessment Tools (<i>n</i> = 6), Embodied and Robotic AI Clinical Simulations (<i>n</i> = 4), AI-Supported Procedural and Technical Skills Training (<i>n</i> = 3), AI-Assisted Clinical Documentation and EHR-Based Skills Training (<i>n</i> = 2), Multimodal Analytics for Skills Assessment (<i>n</i> = 2), Educator-Facing AI Case Authoring and Simulation Design Tools (<i>n</i> = 2), and AI-Supported Clinical Reasoning and Tutoring Tools (<i>n</i> = 1). Publication activity concentrated heavily in 2024-2025, with virtual patient applications representing the dominant category.<h4>Conclusions</h4>AI implementation in clinical skills education has accelerated substantially since 2022, with large language model-powered virtual patient simulations emerging as the predominant application. Current implementations primarily position AI as a supplementary formative tool rather than a replacement for established pedagogical approaches. Robust evidence regarding long-term educational impact remains limited, indicating need for rigorous longitudinal evaluation alongside continued innovation.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Undergraduate Medical Education","Clinical Skills","Virtual Patient Simulation","Scoping Review","Osce Assessment","Generative Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1830254"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290728"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13290728?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"42313226","source":"MED","pmid":"42313226","pmcid":"PMC13279657","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13279657"]},"doi":"10.1007/s11920-026-01690-4","title":"The Digital Mirror: Clinical Potentials and Relational Risks of Generative AI in Mental Health Interventions.","authorString":"Cavalera C, Frisone F, Rossi C, Oasi O, Pagnini F, Riva G, Antichi L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cavalera C","firstName":"Cesare","lastName":"Cavalera","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy. cesarem.cavalera@unicatt.it."}]}},{"fullName":"Frisone F","firstName":"Fabio","lastName":"Frisone","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sophia University Institute, Firenze, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Rossi C","firstName":"Chiara","lastName":"Rossi","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Human Science, Università degli Studi Guglielmo Marconi, Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Oasi O","firstName":"Osmano","lastName":"Oasi","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Pagnini F","firstName":"Francesco","lastName":"Pagnini","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Riva G","firstName":"Giuseppe","lastName":"Riva","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Humane Technology Laboratory, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Applied Technology for Neuro-Psychology Laboratory, Istituto Auxologico Italiano IRCCS, Milan, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Antichi L","firstName":"Lorenzo","lastName":"Antichi","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"28","journalIssueId":4209441,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Current psychiatry reports","medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Psychiatry Rep","isoabbreviation":"Curr Psychiatry Rep","nlmid":"100888960","essn":"1535-1645","issn":"1523-3812"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"40","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose of review</h4>This review explores the rapidly evolving integration of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) in mental health care. It aims to evaluate current applications in assessment, treatment planning, and psychotherapeutic interventions, while critically examining the clinical risks, ethical dilemmas, and the future potential of GenAI as an adjunctive tool rather than a replacement for human-delivered therapy.<h4>Recent findings</h4>Recent studies indicate that AI models can effectively assist in diagnostic reasoning, biomarker identification via EEG, and the prediction of symptom trajectories from session transcripts. Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) suggest that GenAI chatbots significantly reduce anxiety and depressive symptoms in the short term, particularly in settings with limited access to clinicians. However, human-led therapy remains superior in fostering deep emotional engagement and clinical impact. Significant risks identified include the potential for GenAI to foster dependency, reinforce maladaptive schemas or delusional ideation through \"sycophantic\" mirroring, and raise complex ethical-legal challenges regarding the reporting of criminal disclosures. AI represents a transformative adjunctive layer in mental health, offering scalable support for assessment, training, and between-session monitoring. While technological advances in personalization, multimodality, and immersive virtual reality enhance its clinical utility, GenAI lacks the authentic relational depth and\"calibrated mismatches\" essential for autonomy and transformative change. Future integration must prioritize a human-centered, blended approach, where GenAI is strictly supervised by clinicians within a robust ethical and regulatory framework to preserve the essential heart of the therapeutic connection. Research priorities, interim clinical safeguards, and recommendations for navigating the gap between current evidence and real-world adoption need to be defined and implemented.","affiliation":"Department of Psychology, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Via Nirone 15, Milan, 20123, Italy. cesarem.cavalera@unicatt.it.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Psychotherapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Natural Language Processing","Psychotherapy Process","Digital Mental Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Ethics In Ai","Chatbot-delivered Interventions"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11920-026-01690-4"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13279657"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13279657?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-18","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-18"},{"id":"42022146","source":"MED","pmid":"42022146","pmcid":"PMC13095736","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13095736"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1748544","title":"Leveraging chatbots for enhanced decision-making: a comprehensive literature review.","authorString":"Simelane PM, Kittur J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Simelane PM","firstName":"Phiwe M","lastName":"Simelane","initials":"PM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."},{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kittur J","firstName":"Javeed","lastName":"Kittur","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."},{"affiliation":"Engineering Pathways, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1748544","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Chatbots using large language models such as ChatGPT, Google Bard, etc., have become increasingly popular in recent years. The chatbot's ability to simulate conversations with users and process input data and respond based on that information has prompted researchers to investigate the applicability of chatbots in decision-making processes across multiple fields. Current literature has investigated the benefits and challenges of using chatbots in a broad context. This paper presents a systematic literature review of current literature discussing chatbots and decision-making processes, exploring the quality of the decision-making process and user perceptions of using chatbots in the decision-making process.<h4>Methods</h4>This SLR aims to provide a comprehensive view of the disciplinary fields in which chatbot decision-making has been evaluated, how chatbots can be used in various fields, and how it compares to human decision-making. Thirty-six articles from seven databases were reviewed in this paper and categorized into six themes: benefits of using chatbot for decision-making, challenges of chatbot-supported decision-making, ethical considerations of using chatbot-supported decision-making, algorithms/tools used in designing chatbots, human vs. AI decision-making, and chatbot decision-making in different fields.<h4>Results</h4>An analysis of these themes revealed (i) benefits of personalized recommendations in decision-making, (ii) issues with inconsistency in output, (iii) ethical concerns about chatbots using sensitive information to make decisions, (iv) ChatGPT's decision-making is the most studied, and (v) human vs. AI decision-making.<h4>Discussion</h4>The practical and research implications of these findings are further explained in the paper.","affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","systematic-review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["decision-making","Systematic Literature Review","Chatbot","Chatgpt","Llm-Based Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1748544"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13095736"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13095736?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-07"},{"id":"42080195","source":"MED","pmid":"42080195","pmcid":"PMC13129269","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13129269"]},"doi":"10.1177/17562848261441693","title":"Responsible use of large language models in gastroenterology and hepatology.","authorString":"Khanna S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khanna S","firstName":"Sahil","lastName":"Khanna","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4085889,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Therapeutic advances in gastroenterology","medlineAbbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","isoabbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","nlmid":"101478893","essn":"1756-2848","issn":"1756-283X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17562848261441693","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) and related generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems are rapidly entering clinical workflows, including in gastroenterology and hepatology, where text-heavy documentation, guideline-driven care, and high-volume patient messaging create a strong demand for decision support and automation. Deployment raises distinctive risks: hallucinations and unsafe recommendations, automation bias, privacy and confidentiality threats, inequitable care, intellectual property and licensing uncertainty, and unclear allocation of responsibility across clinicians, institutions, and vendors. Regulators are increasingly emphasizing a lifecycle approach to trustworthy AI, including validation, risk management, human oversight, cybersecurity, monitoring, and transparency. Frameworks for healthcare protection regulate how health information may be shared with external model providers and how outputs should be logged, audited, and retained. This review synthesizes practical guidance for responsible LLM use, organized around (1) tiered use cases and risk stratification; (2) ethical principles (beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice, and accountability); (3) core legal and regulatory considerations; and (4) operational governance, evaluation, and monitoring strategies. Actionable checklists to support institutional adoption, use, and transparency are provided. Responsible use requires aligning LLM capabilities with risk, preventing inappropriate data sharing, validating performance in representative populations, ensuring human oversight and clear accountability, mitigating harm, and maintaining an evidence-based, continuously monitored deployment lifecycle.","affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Ethics","Gastroenterology","Hepatology","Law","Privacy","Clinical Decision Support","Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17562848261441693"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"42308509","source":"MED","pmid":"42308509","pmcid":"PMC13324317","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13324317"]},"doi":"10.2196/92240","title":"Ethical Considerations in Personal Health Large Language Models.","authorString":"Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e92240","abstractText":"Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) have rapidly evolved from research prototypes into consumer-facing, data-linked systems that support symptom triage, medication questions, mental health check-ins, and longitudinal self-management. Their direct-to-consumer use without clinical oversight creates a distinct ethical risk profile that general artificial intelligence governance frameworks do not fully address. This viewpoint focuses on text-based, platform-mediated PH-LLMs and synthesizes PH-LLM-specific challenges across 6 domains: privacy, accuracy, equity, transparency, human-artificial intelligence interaction, and regulatory governance. These risks may be amplified by health literacy gaps, longitudinal data aggregation, persuasive conversational design, and fragmented oversight across the consumer-clinical boundary. Grounded in the 4 principles of biomedical ethics, we propose a governance framework that operationalizes beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, and justice through design and deployment controls, including health literacy-aligned communication, crisis and pharmacological safeguards, hallucination mitigation, role disclosure, granular consent, fairness auditing, and accessible design. We further outline implementation mechanisms, including risk-tiered certification, tiered accountability, and postdeployment oversight through adverse-event reporting, transparency reporting, and independent safety evaluation. This framework is intended as an evidence-informed but partly anticipatory approach to governing PH-LLMs in personal health management.","affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Literacy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ethics","Patient Safety","Health Literacy","Privacy","Governance","Health Equity","Digital Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Health Ai","Personal Health Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/92240"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"41202290","source":"MED","pmid":"41202290","pmcid":"PMC12639340","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12639340"]},"doi":"10.2196/69006","title":"Standardizing and Scaffolding Health Care AI-Chatbot Evaluation: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Hua Y, Xia W, Bates D, Hartstein GL, Kim HT, Li M, Nelson BW, Stromeyer Iv C, King D, Suh J, Zhou L, Torous J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hua Y","firstName":"Yining","lastName":"Hua","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7779-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Technology and Operations Management, Harvard Business School, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Xia W","firstName":"Winna","lastName":"Xia","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4352-9398"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Bates D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Bates","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6268-1540"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Internal Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hartstein GL","firstName":"George Luke","lastName":"Hartstein","initials":"GL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3482-3118"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Thomas Jefferson University, Philadelphia, PA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim HT","firstName":"Hyungjin Tom","lastName":"Kim","initials":"HT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5449-3831"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Human Behavior, Brown University, Providence, RI, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Li M","firstName":"Michael","lastName":"Li","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2456-4834"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Technology and Operations Management, Harvard Business School, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Nelson BW","firstName":"Benjamin W","lastName":"Nelson","initials":"BW","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5474-7674"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Stromeyer Iv C","firstName":"Charles","lastName":"Stromeyer Iv","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5957-9258"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"King D","firstName":"Darlene","lastName":"King","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0796-282X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of Texas Southwestern, Houston, TX, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Suh J","firstName":"Jina","lastName":"Suh","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7646-5563"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microsoft (United States), Redmond, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou L","firstName":"Li","lastName":"Zhou","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8170-4549"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Internal Medicine, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Torous J","firstName":"John","lastName":"Torous","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5362-7937"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, MA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6268-1540"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7779-1208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2456-4834"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5362-7937"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5474-7674"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7646-5563"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8170-4549"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0796-282X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3482-3118"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4352-9398"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5957-9258"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-5449-3831"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"4","journalIssueId":4042859,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-11-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR AI","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR AI","isoabbreviation":"JMIR AI","nlmid":"9918645789006676","essn":"2817-1705","issn":"2817-1705"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e69006","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Health care chatbots are rapidly proliferating, while generative artificial intelligence (AI) outpaces existing evaluation standards.<h4>Objective</h4>We aimed to develop a structured, stakeholder-informed framework to standardize evaluation of health care chatbots.<h4>Methods</h4>PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic reviews and Meta-Analyses)-guided searches across multiple databases identified 266 records; 152 were screened, 21 full texts were assessed, and 11 frameworks were included. We extracted 356 questions (refined to 271 by deduplication and relevance review), mapped items to Coalition for Health AI constructs, and organized them with iterative input from clinicians, patients, developers, epidemiologists, and policymakers.<h4>Results</h4>We developed the Health Care AI Chatbot Evaluation Framework (HAICEF), a hierarchical framework with 3 priority domains (safety, privacy, and fairness; trustworthiness and usefulness; and design and operational effectiveness) and 18 second-level and 60 third-level constructs covering 271 questions. Emphasis includes data provenance and harm control; Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act/General Data Protection Regulation-aligned privacy and security; bias management; and reliability, transparency, and workflow integration. Question distribution across domains is as follows: design and operational effectiveness, 40%; trustworthiness and usefulness, 39%; and safety, privacy and fairness, 21%. The framework accommodates both patient-facing and back-office use cases.<h4>Conclusions</h4>HAICEF provides an adaptable scaffold for standardized evaluation and responsible implementation of health care chatbots. Planned next steps include prospective validation across settings and a Delphi consensus to extend accountability and accessibility assurances.","affiliation":"Technology and Operations Management, Harvard Business School, Cambridge, MA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Evaluation Framework","Generative Ai","Large Language Model","Ai Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/69006"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12639340"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12639340?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":4,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-11-07","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-07","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-22","dateOfRevision":"2025-11-25","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-07","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-07"},{"id":"41262650","source":"MED","pmid":"41262650","pmcid":"PMC12623327","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12623327"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2025.1633577","title":"Artificial intelligence in oncology: promise, peril, and the future of patient-physician interaction.","authorString":"Thind BS, Tsao CK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thind BS","firstName":"Birpartap S","lastName":"Thind","initials":"BS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-2993-1016"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Tsao CK","firstName":"Che-Kai","lastName":"Tsao","initials":"CK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Northwell Cancer Institute, New Hyde Park, NY, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-2993-1016"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":3897144,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1633577","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly embedded in oncology. While initial technical evaluations emphasize diagnostic accuracy and efficiency, the impact on patient-physician interaction (PPI)-the foundation of trust, communication, comprehension, and shared decision-making-remains underexplored. In this review, we studied the current development of AI technology facing both physicians and patients with a focus in cancer care. Among different AI technologies, chatbots, large language model agents, and extended reality applications have shown the promise to date. Survey data suggest oncologists recognize AI's potential to augment efficiency but remain cautious about liability and the erosion of relational care. Key to future AI success in improving cancer care critically depends on design, validation, governance, and human guidance and gatekeeping in care delivery.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, California University of Science and Medicine, Colton, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Communication","Oncology","Shared Decision-making","Patient–physician Interaction","Chatbots","Extended Reality"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1633577"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12623327"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12623327?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2025-11-20","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-20","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-20","dateOfRevision":"2025-11-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-04","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-04"},{"id":"42333365","source":"MED","pmid":"42333365","pmcid":"PMC13283513","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13283513"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.109393","title":"Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Hospital Quality Management, Patient Safety, and Accreditation Readiness: A Systematic Review and Narrative Synthesis.","authorString":"Marathe N, Ranjan M, Singh MP, Trigunait P, Prakash A, Kaushal N, Pawar A, Kshatriya GS, Dubey R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Marathe N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Marathe","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjan M","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Ranjan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microbiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jammu, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh MP","firstName":"Madhusudan P","lastName":"Singh","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Trigunait P","firstName":"Pragati","lastName":"Trigunait","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Prakash A","firstName":"Avinash","lastName":"Prakash","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anaesthesiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaushal N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Kaushal","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Medicine and Public Health, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawar A","firstName":"Amit","lastName":"Pawar","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, Military Hospital Doda, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kshatriya GS","firstName":"Girish Singh","lastName":"Kshatriya","initials":"GS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubey R","firstName":"Rounak","lastName":"Dubey","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0295-3262"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4188949,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e109393","abstractText":"As healthcare systems become increasingly data-driven, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are being explored as tools to support quality management, patient safety, and accreditation. This systematic review synthesizes the available evidence across these domains through a structured literature search of major biomedical databases, encompassing studies published over the past decade and narratively synthesized to capture implementation context alongside technical performance. Three electronic databases were searched, and 23 studies were included. The available empirical evidence suggests that these technologies are most useful for repetitive, operationally well-defined, and digitally captured tasks, while local validation, acceptance testing, and continuous monitoring consistently emerge as prerequisites for safe institutional deployment. Governance capacity and organizational readiness are identified as equally determinative of outcomes as algorithmic performance itself. Collectively, the evidence supports positioning AI and ML as augmentative instruments within human-led quality governance structures, rather than as autonomous operational systems. Evidence was heterogeneous, with most empirical studies being single-center, retrospective, or implementation-focused. The risk-of-bias assessment indicated limited high-quality comparative evidence, limiting the strength of the pooled conclusions.","affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Quality assurance","Accreditation","Patient Safety","Machine Learning","Natural Language Processing","Deep Learning","Healthcare Governance","Hospital Quality Management"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.109393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-21"},{"id":"41952677","source":"MED","pmid":"41952677","pmcid":"PMC13053261","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13053261"]},"doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441","title":"Large language models in patient education for brain tumors: opportunities, risks, and ethical considerations.","authorString":"Christodoulou RC, Papageorgiou PS, Lucio Pereira AC, Solomou EE, Papageorgiou SG, Vassiliou E, Georgiou MF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Christodoulou RC","firstName":"Rafail C","lastName":"Christodoulou","initials":"RC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou PS","firstName":"Platon S","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"PS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Lucio Pereira AC","firstName":"Ana Carolina","lastName":"Lucio Pereira","initials":"AC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oncological Research Institute (IPON)/Discipline of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Federal University of Triângulo Mineiro, Uberaba, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Solomou EE","firstName":"Elena E","lastName":"Solomou","initials":"EE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine-Hematology, University of Patras Medical School, Rion, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou SG","firstName":"Sokratis G","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"SG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1st Department of Neurology, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Eginition Hospital, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Vassiliou E","firstName":"Evros","lastName":"Vassiliou","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Kean University, Union, NJ, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Georgiou MF","firstName":"Michalis F","lastName":"Georgiou","initials":"MF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, University of Miami, Miami, FL, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6102-3344"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":4110619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oncol","isoabbreviation":"Front Oncol","nlmid":"101568867","essn":"2234-943X","issn":"2234-943X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1795441","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Patients with brain tumors often struggle to understand their condition because of complex imaging findings, multidisciplinary care pathways, and frequent cognitive and emotional vulnerability. Effective patient education is, therefore, essential but difficult to deliver within routine clinical encounters.<h4>Objective</h4>This narrative review evaluates the role of large language models (LLMs) in supporting patient education for individuals with brain tumors.<h4>Content</h4>We synthesize evidence from neuro-oncology, radiology, and digital health literature on the use of LLMs to explain imaging results, diagnoses, and treatment options in patient-centered language. Potential benefits include improved health literacy, accessibility, and continuity of education. Key limitations are also discussed, including hallucinations, output variability, overtrust, data privacy concerns, and ethical challenges. A clinician-guided framework for responsible integration is proposed.<h4>Conclusion</h4>When used under clinician supervision as educational support tools, LLMs may enhance patient understanding and engagement in brain tumor care. Safe implementation will require structured governance, oversight, and alignment with ethical standards.","affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Brain tumors","Patient education","Neuro-oncology","Artificial Intelligence In Healthcare","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-24"},{"id":"42196638","source":"MED","pmid":"42196638","pmcid":"PMC13206135","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13206135"]},"doi":"10.3390/ijerph23050545","title":"Applications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Breast Cancer Care Delivery and Education: A Scoping Review.","authorString":"Seripenah PN, Ikechukwu P, Oni G, Polotto S, Adeboye W, Leonardi-Bee J, Jordan C, Morling J, Aiyelabegan F, Dhungana S, Emery H, Martello E, Stewart-Evans J, Evans C, Taggar J, Wilson E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Seripenah PN","firstName":"Princella Ntumwine","lastName":"Seripenah","initials":"PN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5565-1255"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Academic Primary Care, School of Medicine, The University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Ikechukwu P","firstName":"Prudence","lastName":"Ikechukwu","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Oni G","firstName":"Georgette","lastName":"Oni","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Trust, Hills Road, Cambridge CB2 0QQ, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Polotto S","firstName":"Susanna","lastName":"Polotto","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nottingham Breast Institute, Nottingham University Hospitals NHS Trust, Nottingham NG5 1PB, UK."},{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Health Science, School of Life Sciences, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Adeboye W","firstName":"William","lastName":"Adeboye","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Trust, Hills Road, Cambridge CB2 0QQ, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Leonardi-Bee J","firstName":"Jo","lastName":"Leonardi-Bee","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Evidence Based Healthcare, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Jordan C","firstName":"Chloe","lastName":"Jordan","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Cambridge University Hospitals NHS Trust, Hills Road, Cambridge CB2 0QQ, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Morling J","firstName":"Joanne","lastName":"Morling","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Evidence Based Healthcare, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Aiyelabegan F","firstName":"Fatimah","lastName":"Aiyelabegan","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8947-3338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Dhungana S","firstName":"Surakshya","lastName":"Dhungana","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Emery H","firstName":"Heidi","lastName":"Emery","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Academic Primary Care, School of Medicine, The University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Martello E","firstName":"Elisa","lastName":"Martello","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Stewart-Evans J","firstName":"James","lastName":"Stewart-Evans","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6213-7905"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Evans C","firstName":"Catrin","lastName":"Evans","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5338-2191"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Evidence Based Healthcare, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Taggar J","firstName":"Jaspal","lastName":"Taggar","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5031-0977"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Academic Primary Care, School of Medicine, The University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Wilson E","firstName":"Emma","lastName":"Wilson","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4695-2184"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."},{"affiliation":"Nottingham Breast Institute, Nottingham University Hospitals NHS Trust, Nottingham NG5 1PB, UK."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Evidence Based Healthcare, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5565-1255"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4695-2184"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5031-0977"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5338-2191"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6213-7905"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8947-3338"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"23","journalIssueId":4201794,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"International journal of environmental research and public health","medlineAbbreviation":"Int J Environ Res Public Health","isoabbreviation":"Int J Environ Res Public Health","nlmid":"101238455","essn":"1660-4601","issn":"1661-7827"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"545","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being applied in breast cancer care, yet its use across the post-diagnosis phase remains poorly mapped. This scoping review aimed to identify and categorise AI applications in post-diagnosis breast cancer care, encompassing treatment planning, treatment delivery, follow-up and surveillance, survivorship, and palliative care. Following JBI methodology and PRISMA-ScR reporting guidelines, four databases (MEDLINE, EMBASE, CINAHL, and Web of Science) were searched, identifying 3784 records. After screening and full-text assessment, 54 studies published between 2016 and 2024 were included. Machine learning was the predominant technology (81%), followed by generative AI (7%), conversational agents (6%), traditional natural language processing (4%), and data mining (2%). Follow-up and surveillance were the most represented care stage (48%), driven primarily by recurrence prediction models. Most applications were provider-focused (83%), while patient-facing tools accounted for 17% of studies and relied on either conversational agents or generative AI. No studies addressed palliative care. The evidence base was predominantly retrospective (70%) and concentrated in high-income countries (74%). Future research should prioritise prospective evaluation in clinical workflows, address unsupervised patient use of generative AI, and ensure equitable development across diverse populations and care settings.","affiliation":"Centre for Public Health and Epidemiology, School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham NG7 2UH, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Scoping Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Breast Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Breast cancer","Patient education","Machine Learning","survivorship","Scoping Review","Recurrence Prediction","Conversational Agents","Post-diagnosis Care","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph23050545"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13206135"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13206135?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-23"},{"id":"42211738","source":"MED","pmid":"42211738","pmcid":"PMC13214549","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13214549"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181","title":"Current themes of AI in mental health: Actionable evidence and guardrails for mood and anxiety care.","authorString":"Paulus MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Paulus MP","firstName":"Martin P","lastName":"Paulus","initials":"MP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Oxley College of Health and Natural Sciences, University of Tulsa, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4182059,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of mood and anxiety disorders","medlineAbbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","isoabbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","nlmid":"9918646056406676","essn":"2950-0044","issn":"2950-0044"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100181","abstractText":"Currently, artificial intelligence (AI) is clinically relevant to mood and anxiety care, but the evidence base is uneven across use cases. This narrative review synthesizes recent literature most relevant to clinicians and investigators. Five themes dominate the current field: patient-facing adjunctive tools, failure modes and safety risks, clinician-facing decision support, passive sensing and measurement infrastructure, and governance. Recent randomized evidence supports a narrow efficacy claim for structured chatbot interventions, with small improvements in depressive and anxiety symptoms and more consistent effects on engagement than on symptom superiority. These studies do not support autonomous psychotherapy, and they do not establish a therapeutic advantage for open-ended large language model systems over more constrained designs. Safety studies, by contrast, identify active concerns: harmful endorsement, weak youth risk assessment, inconsistent crisis handling, and anxiety/OCD reassurance loops. The strongest current clinical signal lies in supervised clinician-facing decision support, where recent trials of AI-assisted antidepressant selection improved treatment persistence and some downstream symptom outcomes. Passive sensing detects behaviorally meaningful signals, but evidence that alert-driven deployment improves care remains insufficient for routine practice. Across stakeholder and policy sources, the most defensible deployment model is human-in-the-loop, stepped, and bounded by explicit handoff rules.","affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Depression","Phenotyping","Implementation","Anxiety","Governance","Chatbot"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-14"},{"id":"41380485","source":"MED","pmid":"41380485","pmcid":"PMC12756637","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12756637"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.identj.2025.109322","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Dentistry: A Concise Review of Reporting Checklists and Guidelines.","authorString":"Khurshid Z, Osathanon T, Shire MA, Schwendicke F, Samaranayake L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khurshid Z","firstName":"Zohaib","lastName":"Khurshid","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Prosthodontics and Dental Implantology, College of Dentistry, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia; Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand."}]}},{"fullName":"Osathanon T","firstName":"Thanaphum","lastName":"Osathanon","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1649-6357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand; Department of Anatomy, Centre of Excellence for Dental Stem Cell Biology, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand. Electronic address: thanaphum.o@chula.ac.th."}]}},{"fullName":"Shire MA","firstName":"Mohamedamin Abdullahi","lastName":"Shire","initials":"MA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Information Technology, NED University of Engineering and Technology, Karachi, Pakistan."}]}},{"fullName":"Schwendicke F","firstName":"Falk","lastName":"Schwendicke","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Conservative Dentistry and Periodontology, LMU University Hospital, LMU Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Samaranayake L","firstName":"Lakshman","lastName":"Samaranayake","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand; Global Research Cell, Dr DY Patil Dental College and Hospital, Dr D Y Patil Vidyapeeth, Pimpri, Pune, 411018, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1649-6357"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"76","journalIssueId":4091606,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"International dental journal","medlineAbbreviation":"Int Dent J","isoabbreviation":"Int Dent J","nlmid":"0374714","essn":"1875-595X","issn":"0020-6539"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"109322","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) has become increasingly integrated into dental diagnostics, imaging and treatment planning. However, despite this growing adoption, adherence to standardised reporting frameworks remains inconsistent. Insufficient use of established checklists continues to impede reproducibility, transparency and regulatory credibility. This review systematically examines existing AI reporting frameworks relevant to dental research, mapping their methodological domains, areas of overlap and persistent implementation gaps. We analysed established medical reporting guidelines for artificial intelligence in healthcare covering trials, protocols, prediction models, bias assessment, decision-support systems, imaging and systematic reviews alongside dentistry-specific checklists and ethical frameworks. While dental AI research is expanding rapidly, its reporting remains fragmented and inconsistent. Existing frameworks provide a comprehensive foundation for transparency and methodological rigour, but are underutilised. Harmonising these frameworks and promoting active adherence through journal policies, regulatory integration and quantitative compliance tracking are essential to bridge the gap between algorithmic performance and trustworthy clinical adoption.","affiliation":"Department of Prosthodontics and Dental Implantology, College of Dentistry, King Faisal University, Al-Ahsa, Saudi Arabia; Center of Artificial Intelligence and Innovation, Faculty of Dentistry, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Chulalongkorn University","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Chulalongkorn University Faculty of Dentistry","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dentistry","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dental Research"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Checklist"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Reproducibility","Transparency","Dentistry","Clinical Validation","Reporting Checklist"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.identj.2025.109322"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12756637"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12756637?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":20,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-14","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-11","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-11","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-11"},{"id":"PPR1207612","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202605.0772.v1","title":"Generative AI-Driven Digital Twin Architecture for Urban Mobility Simulation and Decision Support","authorString":"Vicente-Martínez P, Soria-Olivas E, Chust-Ros A, García-Escrivà MÁ, William-Secin E, Sánchez-Montañés M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Vicente-Martínez P","firstName":"Pablo","lastName":"Vicente-Martínez","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Soria-Olivas E","firstName":"Emilio","lastName":"Soria-Olivas","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9148-8405"}},{"fullName":"Chust-Ros A","firstName":"Adrián","lastName":"Chust-Ros","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-5724-0459"}},{"fullName":"García-Escrivà MÁ","firstName":"María Ángeles","lastName":"García-Escrivà","initials":"MÁ"},{"fullName":"William-Secin E","firstName":"Edu","lastName":"William-Secin","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Sánchez-Montañés M","firstName":"Manuel","lastName":"Sánchez-Montañés","initials":"M"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5944-0532"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9148-8405"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-5724-0459"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-3220-4795"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-6519-392X"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Urban mobility planning in smart cities requires sophisticated simulation tools, yet their complexity often creates a technical barrier for non-expert stakeholders. This paper presents a novel architecture that integrates generative artificial intelligence with digital twin technology to create an accessible and robust decision-support system. The framework employs a conversational AI agent based on Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite to interpret natural language intentions and translate them into validated simulation parameters. A critical safety layer, built using Pydantic, ensures that the agent’s stochastic outputs adhere to strict technical schemas and urban logic before execution. The underlying digital twin, developed with SimPy, NetworkX, and OSMnx, features a multi-source data integration strategy that includes demographic density (INE), tourism activity (ISTAC), and high-resolution traffic statistics (TomTom) to calibrate vehicle behavior. The architecture was validated through a Technology Readiness Level (TRL) 4 proof-of-concept in Las Palmas de Gran Canaria, simulating multimodal scenarios including buses, the future MetroGuagua (BRT), and pedestrian flows. Results demonstrate a 95.99% success rate in intent recognition and configuration mapping, with end-to-end execution times under 20 minutes for a 19-hour simulated day. This study demonstrates that LLM-driven orchestration, coupled with automated data pipelines and a decoupled microservice architecture, can democratize access to urban simulation, fostering more inclusive, agile, and evidence-based smart city governance.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202605.0772.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1207612","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-05-12","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-12"},{"id":"42341243","source":"MED","pmid":"42341243","pmcid":"PMC13293476","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13293476"]},"doi":"10.2196/90709","title":"The Emerging Roles of AI in Self-Directed Stress Management: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Reyes MKG, Teo SSM, Hartanto A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Reyes MKG","firstName":"Mary Kamillah Grace","lastName":"Reyes","initials":"MKG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5470-0516"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}},{"fullName":"Teo SSM","firstName":"Shauna Sha Min","lastName":"Teo","initials":"SSM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3739-8116"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}},{"fullName":"Hartanto A","firstName":"Andree","lastName":"Hartanto","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8758-6400"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8758-6400"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3739-8116"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5470-0516"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e90709","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Stress is widespread and carries substantial mental health, social, and economic burdens. Yet, access to clinician-led stress management remains constrained by service capacity, cost, and stigma. In response, artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled tools have rapidly proliferated as scalable, self-directed options. However, evidence on how these systems support stress management outside formal clinical settings remains fragmented.<h4>Objective</h4>This systematic review aimed to synthesize empirical evidence on how AI-enabled technologies are used for self-directed stress management. We mapped the emerging functions of these tools, the psychological frameworks informing their design, the populations and settings studied, and the outcomes reported.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses)-compliant systematic review of English-language studies published between 2000 and 2025. Six databases were searched (APA PsycINFO, PubMed, MEDLINE, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, ProQuest, and Google Scholar).<h4>Results</h4>Of 3008 records identified, 35 studies met the inclusion criteria. The methodological quality of included studies was critically appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (version 2018). Findings illustrated that AI-supported stress management can operate through 5 core functions, including psychological intervention, behavioral support, psychoeducation, companionship, and emotional support, and stress monitoring, detection, and triage. Across the reviewed studies, these functions supported self-directed stress management by helping users identify stress, regulate responses, and engage in coping outside formal clinical care.<h4>Conclusions</h4>AI-enabled systems show preliminary promise for supporting self-directed stress management through multiple user-facing functions grounded in established psychological frameworks.","affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Stress, Psychological","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Coping Skills"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Systematic review","Stress Management","Prisma","Psychoeducation","Preferred Reporting Items For Systematic Reviews And Meta-analyses","Stress Monitoring","Chatbots","Digital Mental Health","Conversational Agents","Self-guided Intervention","Self-directed Stress Management"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/90709"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293476"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293476?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"42330487","source":"MED","pmid":"42330487","pmcid":"PMC13286327","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13286327"]},"doi":"10.2196/94972","title":"Solidarity or Segregation? ChatGPT Health and US Health Care Disparities.","authorString":"Barnhart AJ, Comerci G, Prainsack B, Braun M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Barnhart AJ","firstName":"Andrew J","lastName":"Barnhart","initials":"AJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7935-253X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Comerci G","firstName":"Giuseppe","lastName":"Comerci","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2520-082X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Prainsack B","firstName":"Barbara","lastName":"Prainsack","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6335-1532"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Political Science, University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria."}]}},{"fullName":"Braun M","firstName":"Matthias","lastName":"Braun","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6687-6027"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6335-1532"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6687-6027"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7935-253X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2520-082X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e94972","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>ChatGPT Health, an artificial intelligence feature launched by OpenAI in January 2026, integrates personal medical records and consumer health data into a large language model-based chatbot. It positions itself as a digital health tool to help users navigate a fragmented US health care system marked by inaccessible insurance, medical deserts, and workforce shortages. We argue that, rather than closing these gaps, ChatGPT Health risks widening what we call the solidarity gap in health care. By appearing to address unmet needs, it may obscure and entrench the structural conditions that produce health care disparities. We identify specific risks to patient safety, such as the legitimization of dangerous self-rationing and self-medication behaviors, inaccurate triage of clinical emergencies, erosion of effective health communication, and the reinforcement of confirmation and anchoring bias. These risks are particularly acute for individuals facing structural barriers to care. To prevent ChatGPT Health and similar large language model-based health tools from deepening inequities, we propose solidarity-based policy interventions such as upstream reforms that rebuild the institutional foundations of access and health equity and downstream safeguards that embed artificial intelligence tools within accountable, equitable health care infrastructures as complements to rather than substitutes for human care.","affiliation":"Department of Social Ethics, University of Bonn, Am Hofgarten 8, Bonn, North Rhine-Westphalia, 53113, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"101076822","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"United States"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Healthcare Disparities"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Solidarity","Health Care Inequity","Digital Health Policy","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/94972"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286327"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286327?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"42369004","source":"MED","pmid":"42369004","pmcid":"PMC13294403","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13294403"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1814651","title":"Agentic AI systems in electrical power systems engineering: current state-of-the-art and challenges.","authorString":"Ghosh S, Mittal G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ghosh S","firstName":"Soham","lastName":"Ghosh","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States."},{"affiliation":"IEEE IAS/PES Kansas City Section, Kansas City, KS, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Mittal G","firstName":"Gaurav","lastName":"Mittal","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1814651","abstractText":"Agentic AI systems have recently emerged as a critical and transformative approach in artificial intelligence, offering capabilities that extend far beyond traditional AI agents and contemporary generative AI models. This rapid evolution necessitates a clear conceptual and taxonomical understanding to differentiate this new paradigm. Our paper addresses this gap by providing a comprehensive review that establishes a precise definition and taxonomy for \"agentic AI,\" with the aim of distinguishing it from previous AI paradigms. The concepts are gradually introduced, starting with a highlight of its diverse applications across the broader field of engineering. The paper then presents four detailed, state-of-the-art use-case applications within electrical power systems engineering, a domain where the impact of agentic AI systems is expected to be particularly significant. The high impact of agentic AI systems in the field of electrical power systems is primarily driven by global trends toward clean energy transition and higher levels of grid automations, all of which create an environment where agentic AI can be readily deployed and effectively leveraged. These case studies demonstrate current and innovative state-of-the-art, ranging from an advanced agentic framework for streamlining complex power system studies and benchmarking to a novel agentic AI system developed for survival analysis of dynamic pricing strategies in battery swapping stations. Finally, robust deployment of these autonomous agents brings a unique set of challenges that are discussed in this manuscript through detailed failure mode investigations. From these findings, we derive actionable recommendations for the design and implementation of safe, reliable, and accountable agentic AI systems, offering a critical resource for researchers and practitioners.","affiliation":"Independent Researcher, Overland Park, Kansas, KS, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Survival analysis","Autonomous Agents","Large Language Model (Llm)","Zero Trust Architecture","Agentic Ai","Model Context Protocol (Mcp)","Agentic Collusion","Power System Automation"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1814651"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13294403"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13294403?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-11"},{"id":"41893075","source":"MED","pmid":"41893075","pmcid":"PMC13028657","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13028657"]},"doi":"10.3390/nursrep16030087","title":"Educational Applications of AI-Based Chatbots in Nursing: A Scoping Review.","authorString":"Fernandes F, Encarnação R, Alves J, Pais-Vieira C, Lima SBS, Alves P.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fernandes F","firstName":"Francisco","lastName":"Fernandes","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-0031-5748"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate Program in Nursing (PPGENF/UFSM), Federal University of Santa Maria, Santa Maria 97105-900, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Encarnação R","firstName":"Rúben","lastName":"Encarnação","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2666-7391"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Interdisciplinary Research in Health (CIIS), Faculty of Health Sciences and Nursing, Catholic University of Portugal, 4169-005 Porto, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Alves J","firstName":"José","lastName":"Alves","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-5809-3788"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Interdisciplinary Research in Health (CIIS), Faculty of Health Sciences and Nursing, Catholic University of Portugal, 4169-005 Porto, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Pais-Vieira C","firstName":"Carla","lastName":"Pais-Vieira","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9262-0375"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Interdisciplinary Research in Health (CIIS), Faculty of Health Sciences and Nursing, Catholic University of Portugal, 4169-005 Porto, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Lima SBS","firstName":"Suzinara Beatriz Soares de","lastName":"Lima","initials":"SBS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2162-8601"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Graduate Program in Nursing (PPGENF/UFSM), Federal University of Santa Maria, Santa Maria 97105-900, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Alves P","firstName":"Paulo","lastName":"Alves","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6348-3316"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Interdisciplinary Research in Health (CIIS), Faculty of Health Sciences and Nursing, Catholic University of Portugal, 4169-005 Porto, Portugal."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2162-8601"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6348-3316"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9262-0375"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2666-7391"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-0031-5748"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-5809-3788"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4161790,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Nursing reports (Pavia, Italy)","medlineAbbreviation":"Nurs Rep","isoabbreviation":"Nurs Rep","nlmid":"101592662","essn":"2039-4403","issn":"2039-439X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"87","abstractText":"<b>Background/Objectives:</b> The rapid expansion of generative artificial intelligence (AI) and large language model-based chatbots has accelerated their adoption in higher education, including nursing. This scoping review mapped the use of AI-based chatbots in nursing education, including curricular domains, pedagogical approaches, educational outcomes, and implementation challenges. <b>Methods:</b> A scoping review was conducted following the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology and reported in accordance with the PRISMA-ScR guideline. Searches were performed across major bibliographic databases and grey literature sources. Quantitative, qualitative, and mixed-methods studies addressing the use of AI chatbots in nursing education or professional training were included. Data were extracted using a standardized instrument and synthesized through descriptive statistics and qualitative content analysis. <b>Results:</b> Sixty-six studies (2019-2025) were included, with significant growth observed after 2023. Most studies employed quasi-experimental designs (37.9%) and were implemented in academic settings (83.3%). Application formats varied across online, hybrid, simulation-based, and classroom models. Reported benefits included improved learning performance, clinical reasoning, and student engagement. Key challenges involved the reliability of AI outputs, academic integrity, data protection, and limited institutional governance. <b>Conclusions:</b> AI-based chatbots represent promising tools to enhance nursing education, particularly when integrated into structured pedagogical strategies with active faculty supervision. Their use can support the development of clinical reasoning, student engagement, and personalized learning. However, methodological heterogeneity, ethical concerns, and governance gaps highlight the need for careful implementation and further rigorous research to ensure safe, effective, and pedagogically sound integration.","affiliation":"Graduate Program in Nursing (PPGENF/UFSM), Federal University of Santa Maria, Santa Maria 97105-900, Brazil.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"0","agency":"FCT - Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia, I.P. by project reference UID/04279/2025 and DOI identifier https://doi.org/10.54499/UID/04279/2025 - Centro de Investigação Interdisciplinar em Saúde.","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UID/04279/2025","agency":"FCT—Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia","orderIn":0},{"agency":"CAPES","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Centro de Investigação Interdisciplinar em Saúde","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nursing Education","Educational Technology","Clinical Reasoning","Scoping Review","Ethics And Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models","Artificial Intelligence Governance"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nursrep16030087"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13028657"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13028657?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-03"},{"id":"42052564","source":"MED","pmid":"42052564","pmcid":"PMC13111155","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13111155"]},"doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s610208","title":"The AI Health Arms Race: A Critical Perspective on Big Tech and the Widening Global Health Equity Gap.","authorString":"Ahmed MM, Othman ZK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ahmed MM","firstName":"Mohamed Mustaf","lastName":"Ahmed","initials":"MM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5991-4052"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, SIMAD University, Mogadishu, Somalia."}]}},{"fullName":"Othman ZK","firstName":"Zhinya Kawa","lastName":"Othman","initials":"ZK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3914-0867"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5991-4052"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3914-0867"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691","essn":"1178-2390","issn":"1178-2390"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"610208","abstractText":"The first quarter of 2026 witnessed an unprecedented convergence, with OpenAI, Anthropic, Microsoft, Google, and Apple launching or advancing dedicated artificial intelligence health platforms. ChatGPT Health, Claude for Healthcare, Copilot Health, Med-Gemini, and Apple Health+ collectively represent a paradigm shift toward AI-mediated personal health management, integrating electronic health records, wearable device data, and conversational AI in privacy-isolated environments. However, these tools are primarily designed for high-income country markets, with limited infrastructure, insufficient multilingual support beyond dominant global languages, and minimal cultural adaptation for low- and middle-income countries. This commentary critically examines the emerging AI health chatbot landscape through the lens of global health equity, analyzing structural barriers, including data poverty, regulatory vacuums, and the risks of data colonialism, whereby large technology corporations extract health data from populations in low- and middle-income countries without proportionate benefit sharing or local capacity building. We propose policy recommendations spanning international governance, national regulatory development, mandatory multilingual content, pre-market clinical safety evaluations, and multilateral financing of digital health infrastructure. We further discuss the strategic responsibilities of both high-income country technology corporations and governments in low- and middle-income countries in bridging this divide. Without deliberate equity-centered governance, the AI health arms race risks widening, rather than narrowing, the global health divide.","affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, SIMAD University, Mogadishu, Somalia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["article-commentary","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Low- And Middle-income Countries","Health Equity","Digital Health","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S610208"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111155"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111155?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-21"},{"id":"42317591","source":"MED","pmid":"42317591","pmcid":"PMC13275148","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13275148"]},"doi":"10.1097/gox.0000000000007841","title":"Beyond Search Engine Optimization: How Large Language Models Are Redefining Surgeon Visibility.","authorString":"Sorenson TJ, Boyd CJ, Hemal K, Cohen O, Karp N, Choi M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sorenson TJ","firstName":"Thomas J","lastName":"Sorenson","initials":"TJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6658-254X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Boyd CJ","firstName":"Carter J","lastName":"Boyd","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Hemal K","firstName":"Kshipra","lastName":"Hemal","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Cohen O","firstName":"Oriana","lastName":"Cohen","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Karp N","firstName":"Nolan","lastName":"Karp","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}},{"fullName":"Choi M","firstName":"Mihye","lastName":"Choi","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6658-254X"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4206291,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Plastic and reconstructive surgery. Global open","medlineAbbreviation":"Plast Reconstr Surg Glob Open","isoabbreviation":"Plast Reconstr Surg Glob Open","nlmid":"101622231","essn":"2169-7574","issn":"2169-7574"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e7841","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs), such as ChatGPT, are rapidly transforming how patients identify and evaluate surgeons, marking the most significant shift in digital patient acquisition since the emergence of search engines. For decades, surgeon visibility online has depended on search engine optimization (SEO), a marketing strategy built around technical website performance, backlinks, and strategic content marketing designed to match keyword-based search behavior. This is in direct contrast to LLMs, which operate as \"recommendation engines\" and synthesize information across vast sources to generate personalized, conversational guidance in response to user queries. Rather than scanning ranked lists of links, patients increasingly can ask nuanced questions and receive narrative, context-sensitive answers. This shift fundamentally alters how expertise is recognized online. LLMs deemphasize traditional SEO signals and instead can emphasize more nuanced information (\"language\"), such as academic affiliation, peer-reviewed scholarship, institutional reputation, high-quality educational writing, and consistency across credible sources. This article outlines how LLMs form surgeon recommendations, why conventional SEO approaches are increasingly insufficient, and what practical steps surgeons can take to strengthen visibility in an artificial intelligence-mediated digital landscape. As generative artificial intelligence becomes embedded into everyday patient information-seeking, surgeons who adapt to this new recommendation paradigm can be best positioned for the next era of online discoverability.","affiliation":"From the Hansjorg Wyss Department of Plastic Surgery, NYU-Langone Health, New York, NY.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/GOX.0000000000007841"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275148"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275148?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"41664164","source":"MED","pmid":"41664164","pmcid":"PMC12896320","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12896320"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13045-026-01781-y","title":"Implementing generative artificial intelligence in precision oncology: safety, governance, and significance.","authorString":"Hamamoto R, Koyama T, Takahashi S, Yasuda T, Kobayashi K, Akagi Y, Kouno N, Sudo K, Hirata M, Sunami K, Kubo T, Katayama H, Takashima A, Taniguchi T, Matsumoto H, Shibaki R, Asada K, Komatsu M, Kaneko S, Yamada M, Horinouchi H, Tanaka K, Goto Y, Kato K, Saito Y, Nakamura K, Yamamoto N.","authorList":{"author":[{